for more than three months russia's  invasion of ukraine has produced  harrowing images of war and suffering  now a group of ukrainian graphic artists  is responding to the violence with  creativity they've come together to  share their work and raise money for  their country face the nation moderator  and chief foreign affairs correspondent  mark brennan has been speaking to some  of the artists about their powerful  images  we are not  soldiers or we are not doctors being an  artist during the war like you think  people are dying in your drawing artist  anna sarvira does not know when she will  return to ukraine she's haunted by the  war you don't have an experience of the  word only if you hate hate and hate and  it's a terrible feeling i was drawn in  this feeling and when you feel only that  you cannot do anything else anna  co-founded a collective to share graphic  art from fellow illustrators most of  them decided not to leave the country  they were never stopping working and  drawing like artist alexander chatokin  as russian bombs dropped on his city he  sent ana works of art each day when i  saw this  it made me tear up he was trying to  capture all the child victims in one  picture anna's collective helped  organize more than 40 exhibitions around  the world to auction artwork and raise  money for ukraine  including at ukraine house here in  washington many of the women have left  they're refugees  anna here ana sarvira is one of them she  is now in germany  this is putin again i call it my secret  dream and i think that hope that this  war  against ukraine will end up to put him  like that with his stash he starts  something terrible miro you never  thought you could hate so much i always  felt like i'm a nice good person  illustrator tanya yacunova's work is  inspired by the bravery of ordinary  ukrainian  we have these videos where russian  shooting  and people don't even scare of the sound  of the gun  she drew even while fleeing the bombing  of keefe  and is now documenting the horrific  atrocities left behind she calls this  piece bucha mother this image is a  collective image of the several victims  who they can share their stories  unfortunately army found corpses of  several young women  naked and raped on a roadside  and russian tried to burn them tanya  used to illustrate children's books but  the war has robbed her and so many  others of that innocence  there is more stories about a woman who  was raped  in front of her small  baby  and neither woman neither  nor baby survived they were shot i felt  so much hate for people who did it this  is probably the hardest piece i ever  draw these women can't tell their  stories but you're telling their stories  i wish i would  i would never need to process  for cbs morning's margaret brennan in  washington  it is so hard to think that  november of last year this was an intact  country i mean it was fearing the worst  but and now all of this  hatred  destruction it's been so haunting to see  the winter in that country which is so  grim in terms of the spring and it looks  so beautiful but the news just gets  worse and worse it gets worse and these  people have to deal with it and it's  it's hard to watch it is hard to live  harder to live through i am sure  unfortunately this art is necessary  decades centuries from now to tell the  story of what happened to remind people  of the atrocities really nice piece for  margaret brown absolutely people cannot  forget this cannot forget what happened
 

